1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
 In this video, we're going to talk about powder metallurgy and metal 3D printing.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:08,000
 Powder metallurgy is an umbrella term for a range of manufacturing processes that,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,680
 as the name suggests, all involve making components from metal powders.

4
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:14,080
 These processes all kind of work the same way.

5
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:17,920
 A first, a metal powder is loaded into a dye and usually compressed.

6
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:21,280
 The constituent granules that make up this powder can all be of a single metal

7
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,160
 or could be a mixture of a bunch of different discrete metals.

8
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,600
 Or they could be granules, particles that themselves are alloys of metals.

9
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,960
 In either case, this powder is loaded into the dye with fillers and binders

10
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,360
 and other additives and then compressed under pressure to consolidate it into shape.

11
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,320
 This pressure lightly binds all of the powder particles together

12
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,400
 for me what's called a green component.

13
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,760
 And while the component that comes out of the dye looks an awful lot like a finished part,

14
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,080
 the granules, the particles that make it up are only lightly held together by electrostatic

15
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:49,360
 and friction forces.

16
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:52,400
 This is where the second step of powder metallurgy processes come in.

17
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:56,400
 The green components are loaded into an oven and underheat all of the particles

18
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:00,240
 melt together and form a continuous reasonably homogenous solid.

19
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:02,960
 Because the powder is forced into the exact shape of the dye,

20
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:06,720
 high dimensional accuracy and service finishes can be achieved through powder metallurgy.

21
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:08,720
 This process is very efficient at scale.

22
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:11,840
 It can be highly automated and there's very little wastage of material.

23
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:15,920
 And complicated dimension critical features like teeth on spurge is for example

24
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:20,800
 that would otherwise need to be machined can be built into this process with no additional cost.

25
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:24,480
 Powder metallurgy processes are limited largely by the mechanisms involved in

26
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:27,360
 loading the dye and then opening it up to get the party out again.

27
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:31,920
 For this reason some complicated or re-entrant features can't be made using this technique.

28
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,880
 There's also considerations that have to be made to achieve in the pressure required

29
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,880
 to consolidate powder metallurgy parts.

30
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,840
 For this reason very large parts can't usually be made by this technique

31
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:42,560
 because you require enormous pressures to consolidate them.

32
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:45,840
 And geometries which vary a lot in the direction of pressing can be tricky as well

33
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:49,920
 because you need to try and achieve a uniform, roughly uniform distribution of pressure

34
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:51,680
 throughout the part when you consolidate it.

35
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:55,200
 Getting any of these things wrong and sometimes even if you get all of these things right

36
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,360
 can lead to porosity inside of the part.

37
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:01,440
 Here even after centering there remain pores, vacancies left inside of the part.

38
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,800
 These like the pores in some ceramics act as nice places for cracks to grow

39
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:08,800
 particularly with load and intention and so generally the kind of thing you want to avoid.

40
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,320
 Generally speaking issues around porosity are managed by making parts using this technique

41
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:16,240
 that don't vary a whole lot in the z direction and working with reasonably thin parts

42
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,240
 so you can achieve a roughly equal and sufficiently high pressure distribution inside

43
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:23,280
 to consolidate it together without introducing porosity.

44
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:27,040
 A related manufacturing process that's really picked up steam over the last couple of years

45
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:30,240
 is metal 3D printing or selective laser centering.

46
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:34,560
 Here a bit of powder is laid down and a laser is used to selectively trace out a cross section

47
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,880
 of the shape you'd like to build over the surface.

48
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:40,960
 This process is a little bit like how an old CRT TV worked or a cathode ray oscilloscope.

49
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:44,160
 The laser kind of moves across the powder drawing out the cross section you like

50
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:46,960
 and in doing so kind of bonds that to the layer before.

51
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,920
 Upon finishing one cross section a new layer of powder is laid down

52
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:51,440
 the process is repeated.

53
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:54,240
 As with regular powder metallurgy this is a two step process.

54
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,320
 At the end of the selective laser centering phase you need to then take your component and put it

55
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:02,240
 in an oven and center it for real to get all the granules to melt together and there are a bunch

56
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:06,400
 of other considerations that you need to keep in mind when metal 3D printing apart particularly

57
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:10,800
 around anisotropy. These considerations are largely analogous to those for plastic 3D printing

58
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,800
 and so we'll talk about all of this together when we meet that technique in a couple of weeks time.

59
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:18,560
 For now what you should remember is that powder metallurgy is an excellent technique for making

60
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:23,760
 small parts very very accurately very very economically and that metal 3D printing can be used

61
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:27,920
 to achieve some really complex and interesting geometries but has some considerations that you need

62
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:30,880
 to think about and again we'll talk about those in a couple of weeks.

63
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:34,720
 In the video description I'll link to a particular good video by Papa darkest Racing talking

64
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:38,480
 about building up an incandale exhaust header using metal 3D printing.

65
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:42,720
 They talk about all the considerations for the design process from 3D scanning the engine bay

66
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:46,560
 to CAD modeling the exhaust headers to the limitations that conventional manufacturing would

67
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:50,080
 place to the freedom from these limitations that metal 3D printing provides and some of the

68
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:53,680
 considerations that have to be made when you do it. If you ask me that video was really a master

69
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:56,880
 class in terms of engineering design it's got a little bit of everything and so I think it's

70
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:57,840
 definitely worth a watch.
